WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.889
Force questions become far clearer when you stop staring at each push separately and start asking for the overall effect.

2
00:00:04.889 --> 00:00:09.778
That overall leftover effect is the resultant force.

3
00:00:09.778 --> 00:00:14.668
If forces act in the same direction, they add.

4
00:00:14.668 --> 00:00:19.557
If they act in opposite directions, you compare them, subtract the smaller from the larger, and keep the direction of the larger side.

5
00:00:19.557 --> 00:00:24.446
Then you ask one more question: is the resultant force zero or non-zero?

6
00:00:24.446 --> 00:00:29.335
That tells you whether the velocity can stay unchanged or must start changing.

7
00:00:29.335 --> 00:00:34.224
Keep this takeaway in view: The target is not every force separately.

8
00:00:34.224 --> 00:00:39.113
The target is the one resultant force left after the directions are accounted for.

9
00:00:39.113 --> 00:00:44.003
Same direction means add.

10
00:00:44.003 --> 00:00:48.892
Forces helping the same way combine into a larger resultant.

11
00:00:48.892 --> 00:00:53.781
Opposite directions means subtract.

12
00:00:53.781 --> 00:00:58.670
Keep the direction of the larger side after subtraction.

13
00:00:58.670 --> 00:01:03.919
Start with the easy combination.

14
00:01:03.919 --> 00:01:09.168
If two forces both act to the right, they are not competing.

15
00:01:09.168 --> 00:01:14.417
They are working together, so you add their sizes.

16
00:01:14.417 --> 00:01:19.666
A 4 newton force to the right and a 7 newton force to the right give a resultant of 11 newtons to the right.

17
00:01:19.666 --> 00:01:24.914
The direction is kept because both original forces already agreed on that direction.

18
00:01:24.914 --> 00:01:30.163
This case matters because it shows that direction is part of the arithmetic, not just an afterthought.

19
00:01:30.163 --> 00:01:35.412
Keep this takeaway in view: Same-direction forces combine by addition because they are building one stronger overall push or pull.

20
00:01:35.412 --> 00:01:40.661
4 N right: One rightward pull.

21
00:01:40.661 --> 00:01:45.910
7 N right: A second rightward pull that helps the first.

22
00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:51.659
Opposite forces are more like a tug-of-war.

23
00:01:51.659 --> 00:01:57.408
If one side pulls harder, there is a leftover pull in that direction.

24
00:01:57.408 --> 00:02:03.157
So you do not add the sizes.

25
00:02:03.157 --> 00:02:08.906
You subtract the smaller from the larger and keep the larger direction.

26
00:02:08.906 --> 00:02:14.655
For example, 10 newtons right and 6 newtons left give 4 newtons right as the resultant force.

27
00:02:14.655 --> 00:02:20.404
The arithmetic is subtraction, but the physics meaning is much deeper: one side is winning overall.

28
00:02:20.404 --> 00:02:26.153
Keep this takeaway in view: Opposite-force subtraction is really a direction comparison.

29
00:02:26.153 --> 00:02:31.902
The bigger side decides the leftover force.

30
00:02:31.902 --> 00:02:37.651
Compare first: Check whether the forces really oppose each other before choosing the arithmetic.

31
00:02:37.651 --> 00:02:43.400
Subtract second: Use larger minus smaller, not total size, when the directions oppose.

32
00:02:43.400 --> 00:02:48.604
This lesson also corrects a very common misunderstanding.

33
00:02:48.604 --> 00:02:53.807
Balanced forces do not mean the object must be stationary.

34
00:02:53.807 --> 00:02:59.011
Balanced forces mean the resultant force is zero.

35
00:02:59.011 --> 00:03:04.215
Zero resultant force means zero acceleration.

36
00:03:04.215 --> 00:03:09.418
If the object was already at rest, it can remain at rest.

37
00:03:09.418 --> 00:03:14.622
If it was already moving at constant velocity, it can keep moving at that same constant velocity.

38
00:03:14.622 --> 00:03:19.825
So balanced forces mean no change in velocity, not no velocity.

39
00:03:19.825 --> 00:03:25.029
Keep this takeaway in view: Balanced forces mean no acceleration.

40
00:03:25.029 --> 00:03:30.233
They do not tell you whether the current velocity is zero or non-zero.

41
00:03:30.233 --> 00:03:35.436
At rest: Zero resultant force can describe a stationary object that stays stationary.

42
00:03:35.436 --> 00:03:40.640
Steady motion: Zero resultant force can also describe an object that keeps moving at constant velocity.

43
00:03:40.640 --> 00:03:46.597
The tug-of-war analogy deserves to stay in memory because it gives force questions a physical feel.

44
00:03:46.597 --> 00:03:52.553
Equal teams give no leftover pull, which matches zero resultant force.

45
00:03:52.553 --> 00:03:58.510
Unequal teams give a leftover pull in the stronger direction, which matches a non-zero resultant force.

46
00:03:58.510 --> 00:04:04.467
The analogy is helpful because it keeps direction visible from the very start.

47
00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:10.423
It stops students from treating forces like unsigned numbers floating in space.

48
00:04:10.423 --> 00:04:16.380
Keep this takeaway in view: Tug-of-war is not just a picture.

49
00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:22.337
It is the logic of resultant force made visible.

50
00:04:22.337 --> 00:04:28.293
Equal teams: No overall winner, so the resultant force is zero.

51
00:04:28.293 --> 00:04:34.250
Stronger side: Leaves a leftover pull in that direction.

52
00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:39.317
Take a cart pulled with 10 newtons to the right and 6 newtons to the left.

53
00:04:39.317 --> 00:04:44.384
Because the forces oppose each other, subtract 6 from 10 to get 4.

54
00:04:44.384 --> 00:04:49.451
Keep the direction of the larger force, so the resultant is 4 newtons to the right.

55
00:04:49.451 --> 00:04:54.518
Because the resultant force is non-zero, the cart accelerates to the right.

56
00:04:54.518 --> 00:04:59.585
That last sentence matters because the lesson wants you to connect the resultant force to motion change, not stop after the subtraction.

57
00:04:59.585 --> 00:05:04.652
Keep this takeaway in view: Find the resultant first, then use it to talk honestly about the motion change.

58
00:05:04.652 --> 00:05:09.719
Rightward pull means 10 N.

59
00:05:09.719 --> 00:05:14.786
This is the larger side of the contest.

60
00:05:14.786 --> 00:05:19.853
Leftward pull means 6 N.

61
00:05:19.853 --> 00:05:24.920
This opposes the rightward pull.

62
00:05:24.920 --> 00:05:28.808
Check the force logic quickly.

63
00:05:28.808 --> 00:05:32.697
What happens to same-direction forces?

64
00:05:32.697 --> 00:05:36.585
They add.

65
00:05:36.585 --> 00:05:40.474
What happens to opposite forces?

66
00:05:40.474 --> 00:05:44.362
Subtract the smaller from the larger and keep the larger direction.

67
00:05:44.362 --> 00:05:48.251
What does zero resultant force mean?

68
00:05:48.251 --> 00:05:52.139
Zero acceleration, which allows rest or constant velocity.

69
00:05:52.139 --> 00:05:56.028
Those three ideas are the backbone of this whole lesson.

70
00:05:56.028 --> 00:05:59.916
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example only asks you to do clearly what these three rules already describe.

71
00:05:59.916 --> 00:06:03.805
Same direction means add.

72
00:06:03.805 --> 00:06:07.693
Build one larger shared-direction force.

73
00:06:07.693 --> 00:06:11.582
Opposite directions means subtract.

74
00:06:11.582 --> 00:06:15.470
Keep the direction of the larger side after subtraction.

75
00:06:15.470 --> 00:06:20.603
You are ready for the worked example.

76
00:06:20.603 --> 00:06:25.736
A cart has 10 newtons to the right and 6 newtons to the left.

77
00:06:25.736 --> 00:06:30.869
Because the forces oppose, subtract 6 from 10 to get 4.

78
00:06:30.869 --> 00:06:36.001
Keep the direction of the larger side, so the resultant force is 4 newtons to the right.

79
00:06:36.001 --> 00:06:41.134
Then interpret the motion.

80
00:06:41.134 --> 00:06:46.267
Because the resultant force is non-zero, the cart accelerates to the right.

81
00:06:46.267 --> 00:06:51.400
That is the full chain: compare directions, combine forces correctly, then decide what the resultant means for the motion.

82
00:06:51.400 --> 00:06:56.533
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: combine the forces correctly, then let the resultant tell the motion story.

83
00:06:56.533 --> 00:07:01.666
Step 1: Decide whether the forces act together or oppose each other.

84
00:07:01.666 --> 00:07:06.799
Step 2: Combine them to get one resultant force with direction.

85
00:07:06.799 --> 00:07:11.931
resultant force = 10 N right - 6 N left = 4 N right.

86
00:07:11.931 --> 00:07:17.064
non-zero resultant -> acceleration to the right.

87
00:07:17.064 --> 00:07:22.197
Do the force combination first.

88
00:07:22.197 --> 00:07:27.330
Then interpret the motion change.
